      "updated": 1781617129784,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) PSP Integration: \n- If a company can safely store sensitive payment information and chooses to do so, PSP can be\nintegrated using API. The company is responsible for developing the payment web pages, collecting\nand storing sensitive payment information. PSP is responsible for connecting to banks or card\nschemes.\n- If a company chooses not to store sensitive payment information due to complex regulations\nand security concerns, PSP provides a hosted payment page to collect card payment details and\nsecurely store them in PSP. This is the approach most companies take.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n2) Reconciliation: \n- It mitigates the risks of dropped asynchronous messages by periodically comparing internal\nsystem states (Ledger vs. Wallet) against nightly external bank/PSP settlement files to\nguarantee financial consistency.\n- When mismatches occur, they are categorized into three resolution paths: automated\nprogrammatic fixes (for known, easily coded issues), manual finance team adjustments (for known\nbut costly-to-automate issues), and manual investigation (for unknown anomalies).\n\n3) Handling Payment Processing Delays: \n- Problem\n    > While most payments complete in seconds, some can stall for hours or days.\n    > due to high risk transaction, extra verifications, poor network connectivity causing timeout. \n- Primary Resolution Strategy (Webhook Push Model)\n    > The PSP immediately returns a PENDING status.\n    > The Client UI displays the pending state to the user and provides a status-checking page.\n    > The PSP tracks the payment asynchronously on its end.\n    > Once finalized (Success/Failure), the PSP notifies the backend Payment Service via a\nregistered Webhook.\n    > The Payment Service updates its internal database and triggers fulfillment (e.g., shipping the\norder).\n- Alternative Resolution Strategy (Polling Pull Model):\n    > If the chosen PSP does not support webhook notifications, the backend Payment Service\nmust actively and periodically poll the PSP's API to fetch status updates for any stuck\nPENDING orders.\n- Another option these days is it gets failed immediately, if any payment gets deducted its\nsorted out via reconciliation and the user can try a new payment. \n\n4) Communication among internal Services\n- Synchronous Communication : Simple to design but creates tight coupling and brittle dependency\nchains. It suffers from poor failure isolation (if one node fails, the request fails) and cascading\nlatency (one slow service degrades the entire system).\n- Asynchronous Communication (e.g., Message Queues / Kafka): The preferred standard for large-\nscale resilience. It operates via Single Receivers (standard queues where one service processes and\ndeletes a message) or Multiple Receivers (pub/sub where a single event independently triggers\nparallel side-effects across analytics, billing, and notification services).\n\n5) Handling Failed Payments(Fault Tolerance)\n- Tracking Payment State:\n    > The system must maintain a definitive state at every stage of the lifecycle.\n    > State changes are persisted in an append-only database table.\n    > This guarantees the system knows exactly where a failure occurred and can definitively\ndecide whether to trigger a retry or a refund.\n- Queueing Strategy (Retry & DLQ):\n    > Non-Retryable (e.g., invalid card number, insufficient funds): Do not retry. Store the error\ndirectly in the database.\n    > Retryable (e.g., transient network timeout, PSP rate limit): Route the event to a Retry\nQueue.\n- Dead Letter Queue (DLQ) Purpose: Acts as a quarantine for persistently failing messages.\nThese are isolated for manual investigation and debugging by engineers or finance teams (e.g.,\nUber utilizes Kafka for this exact fault-tolerance pattern).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n6) Exactly-Once Delivery: Prevent the most severe payment error: double-charging a customer.\n- Core Equation: Exactly-Once = At-Least-Once + At-Most-Once\n- At-Least-Once Guarantee (Retries): \n    > Purpose: Ensures the transaction eventually succeeds despite transient network drops or\ntimeouts.\n    > Common Retry Strategies:\n        Immediate: Resend instantly.\n        Fixed Interval: Wait a set time (e.g., 5s) between attempts.\n        Exponential Backoff (Recommended Standard): Double the wait time after each failure (1s -\n> 2s -> 4s -> 8s). Prevents DDoS-ing your own servers during an outage.\n        Cancel: Abort entirely if the failure is permanent (e.g., card declined).\n    > The Risk: Retries create the exact scenario for double payments. (e.g., A payment succeeds\nat the PSP, but the \"Success\" response gets dropped by the network. The client retries the\nrequest, potentially charging the card a second time).\n- At-Most-Once Guarantee (Idempotency): \n    > Idempotency ensures an operation yields the exact same result regardless of how many times\nit is executed, preventing duplicate actions.\n    > Client-to-Backend (DB Constraints): The client sends a unique UUID in the header\n(idempotency-key). The backend relies on database Unique Primary Key constraints to reject\nduplicate inserts and safely return the cached status.\n    > Backend-to-PSP (Tokens): The backend passes a unique nonce or token to the PSP. If a\nnetwork retry occurs, the PSP recognizes the token, preventing a double-charge and returning the\noriginal execution status.\n\n7) Consistency: \n- To maintain data consistency between internal services, ensuring exactly-once processing is very\nimportant.\n- To maintain data consistency between the internal service and external service (PSP), we usually\nrely on idempotency and reconciliation. If the external service supports idempotency, we should use\nthe same idempotency key for payment retry operations. Even if an external service supports\nidempotent API, reconciliation is still needed because we shouldn’t assume the external system is\nalways right.\n- If data is replicated, replication lag could cause inconsistent data between the primary database\nand the replicas. There are generally two options to solve this:\n    > Serve both reads and writes from the primary database only. This approach is easy to set\nup, but the obvious drawback is scalability. \n    > Ensure all replicas are always in-sync. We could use consensus algorithms \n\n8) Payment Security\n- Eavesdropping: Use HTTPS to encrypt all request and response traffic.\n- Data Tampering: Enforce robust encryption and strict integrity monitoring.\n- Man-in-the-Middle Attacks: Use SSL with Certificate Pinning to guarantee the client is\ncommunicating with the legitimate server.\n- Data Loss: Implement Multi-Region Database Replication and take regular Data Snapshots for\ndisaster recovery.\n- DDoS Attacks: Deploy Rate Limiting and Firewalls to block malicious traffic spikes and prevent\nsystem overload.\n- Card Theft: Use Tokenization (store secure, opaque tokens instead of raw 16-digit card\nnumbers).\n- Fraud: Utilize Address Verification, CVV checks, and User Behavior Analysis to detect and block\nsuspicious actors.",
      "fontSize": 19.47692467305834,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) PSP Integration: \n- If a company can safely store sensitive payment information and chooses to do so, PSP can be integrated using API. The company is responsible for developing the payment web pages, collecting and storing sensitive payment information. PSP is responsible for connecting to banks or card schemes.\n- If a company chooses not to store sensitive payment information due to complex regulations and security concerns, PSP provides a hosted payment page to collect card payment details and securely store them in PSP. This is the approach most companies take.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n2) Reconciliation: \n- It mitigates the risks of dropped asynchronous messages by periodically comparing internal system states (Ledger vs. Wallet) against nightly external bank/PSP settlement files to guarantee financial consistency.\n- When mismatches occur, they are categorized into three resolution paths: automated programmatic fixes (for known, easily coded issues), manual finance team adjustments (for known but costly-to-automate issues), and manual investigation (for unknown anomalies).\n\n3) Handling Payment Processing Delays: \n- Problem\n    > While most payments complete in seconds, some can stall for hours or days.\n    > due to high risk transaction, extra verifications, poor network connectivity causing timeout. \n- Primary Resolution Strategy (Webhook Push Model)\n    > The PSP immediately returns a PENDING status.\n    > The Client UI displays the pending state to the user and provides a status-checking page.\n    > The PSP tracks the payment asynchronously on its end.\n    > Once finalized (Success/Failure), the PSP notifies the backend Payment Service via a registered Webhook.\n    > The Payment Service updates its internal database and triggers fulfillment (e.g., shipping the order).\n- Alternative Resolution Strategy (Polling Pull Model):\n    > If the chosen PSP does not support webhook notifications, the backend Payment Service must actively and periodically poll the PSP's API to fetch status updates for any stuck PENDING orders.\n- Another option these days is it gets failed immediately, if any payment gets deducted its sorted out via reconciliation and the user can try a new payment. \n\n4) Communication among internal Services\n- Synchronous Communication : Simple to design but creates tight coupling and brittle dependency chains. It suffers from poor failure isolation (if one node fails, the request fails) and cascading latency (one slow service degrades the entire system).\n- Asynchronous Communication (e.g., Message Queues / Kafka): The preferred standard for large-scale resilience. It operates via Single Receivers (standard queues where one service processes and deletes a message) or Multiple Receivers (pub/sub where a single event independently triggers parallel side-effects across analytics, billing, and notification services).\n\n5) Handling Failed Payments(Fault Tolerance)\n- Tracking Payment State:\n    > The system must maintain a definitive state at every stage of the lifecycle.\n    > State changes are persisted in an append-only database table.\n    > This guarantees the system knows exactly where a failure occurred and can definitively decide whether to trigger a retry or a refund.\n- Queueing Strategy (Retry & DLQ):\n    > Non-Retryable (e.g., invalid card number, insufficient funds): Do not retry. Store the error directly in the database.\n    > Retryable (e.g., transient network timeout, PSP rate limit): Route the event to a Retry Queue.\n- Dead Letter Queue (DLQ) Purpose: Acts as a quarantine for persistently failing messages. These are isolated for manual investigation and debugging by engineers or finance teams (e.g., Uber utilizes Kafka for this exact fault-tolerance pattern).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n6) Exactly-Once Delivery: Prevent the most severe payment error: double-charging a customer.\n- Core Equation: Exactly-Once = At-Least-Once + At-Most-Once\n- At-Least-Once Guarantee (Retries): \n    > Purpose: Ensures the transaction eventually succeeds despite transient network drops or timeouts.\n    > Common Retry Strategies:\n        Immediate: Resend instantly.\n        Fixed Interval: Wait a set time (e.g., 5s) between attempts.\n        Exponential Backoff (Recommended Standard): Double the wait time after each failure (1s -> 2s -> 4s -> 8s). Prevents DDoS-ing your own servers during an outage.\n        Cancel: Abort entirely if the failure is permanent (e.g., card declined).\n    > The Risk: Retries create the exact scenario for double payments. (e.g., A payment succeeds at the PSP, but the \"Success\" response gets dropped by the network. The client retries the request, potentially charging the card a second time).\n- At-Most-Once Guarantee (Idempotency): \n    > Idempotency ensures an operation yields the exact same result regardless of how many times it is executed, preventing duplicate actions.\n    > Client-to-Backend (DB Constraints): The client sends a unique UUID in the header (idempotency-key). The backend relies on database Unique Primary Key constraints to reject duplicate inserts and safely return the cached status.\n    > Backend-to-PSP (Tokens): The backend passes a unique nonce or token to the PSP. If a network retry occurs, the PSP recognizes the token, preventing a double-charge and returning the original execution status.\n\n7) Consistency: \n- To maintain data consistency between internal services, ensuring exactly-once processing is very important.\n- To maintain data consistency between the internal service and external service (PSP), we usually rely on idempotency and reconciliation. If the external service supports idempotency, we should use the same idempotency key for payment retry operations. Even if an external service supports idempotent API, reconciliation is still needed because we shouldn’t assume the external system is always right.\n- If data is replicated, replication lag could cause inconsistent data between the primary database and the replicas. There are generally two options to solve this:\n    > Serve both reads and writes from the primary database only. This approach is easy to set up, but the obvious drawback is scalability. \n    > Ensure all replicas are always in-sync. We could use consensus algorithms \n\n8) Payment Security\n- Eavesdropping: Use HTTPS to encrypt all request and response traffic.\n- Data Tampering: Enforce robust encryption and strict integrity monitoring.\n- Man-in-the-Middle Attacks: Use SSL with Certificate Pinning to guarantee the client is communicating with the legitimate server.\n- Data Loss: Implement Multi-Region Database Replication and take regular Data Snapshots for disaster recovery.\n- DDoS Attacks: Deploy Rate Limiting and Firewalls to block malicious traffic spikes and prevent system overload.\n- Card Theft: Use Tokenization (store secure, opaque tokens instead of raw 16-digit card numbers).\n- Fraud: Utilize Address Verification, CVV checks, and User Behavior Analysis to detect and block suspicious actors.",
