      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n1) Functional Requirements:  \n- System should handle a pay-in flow (receive money from customers on behalf of sellers).\n- System should handle a pay-out flow (send money to sellers globally).\n- System must accurately track execution status of payment orders (NOT_STARTED,\nEXECUTING, SUCCESS, FAILED).\n\n---- Out of scope ------\n- Product search, shopping cart management, and order fulfillment.\n- Identity verification and KYC (Know Your Customer) processes.\n- Currency conversion processing (assumed handled by PSP).\n\n2) Scale Understanding \n- 1 million payment transactions per day.\n\n2) Non Functional Requirements (SPARCS):  \n- Scalability: System should easily handle 1M transactions/day and scale securely for peak e-\ncommerce loads.\n- Performance: Focus is less on extreme low-latency throughput, and more on exact precision\n(e.g., using \"string\" data types for currency amounts to avoid rounding errors).\n- Availability: Highly available backend to accept checkout requests without dropping orders.\n- Reliability: Robust fault tolerance. Failed payments must be carefully handled and\nretried/logged without data loss.\n- Consistency: Strict ACID transaction support (Traditional Relational DB preferred over\nNoSQL). Asynchronous reconciliation process to keep internal services (accounting) and\nexternal services (PSPs) consistent.\n- Security: End-to-end traceability via an immutable Double-Entry Ledger system (sum of all\nentries must always equal 0).\n\n------- Out of Scope --------\n- Fraud detection and prevention.\n\n3) BOE Calculations \n- QPS\n    > Read QPS : 2*(1M transactions)/ (10^5) => 20 QPS\n    > Write QPS : 1*(1M transactions)/ (10^5) => 10 QPS\n    > Peak QPS ≈ 30 QPS (Write) / 60 QPS (Read)\n\n4) Core Entities\n- PaymentEvent (Tracks checkout_id, buyer/seller/card info, is_payment_done status)\n- PaymentOrder (Tracks payment_order_id, amount, currency, execution status, and\nledger/wallet update flags)\n- Ledger (Double-entry system tracking Account, Debit, and Credit)\n\n5) API Design \n\nPOST /v1/payments -- Executes a payment event handling\n    Input: {buyer_info, checkout_id, credit_card_info, payment_orders}\n    Output: 2xx, success / accepted\n\nGET /v1/payments/:payment_order_id -- real-time execution status of payment order.\n    Output: {payment_order_id, execution_status, ledger_updated, wallet_updated, \nfailure_reason}\n\nPOST /v1/webhooks/psp-update -- asynchronous status updates from third-party PSP\n    Input: {event_type, psp_reference_id, payment_order_id, status}\n    Output: 200, success\n\nPOST /v1/payments/:payment_order_id/refund -- refund via a reverse double-entry ledger\nupdate\n    Input: {refund_amount, reason}\n    Output: 2xx, accepted\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n1) Functional Requirements:  \n- System should handle a pay-in flow (receive money from customers on behalf of sellers).\n- System should handle a pay-out flow (send money to sellers globally).\n- System must accurately track execution status of payment orders (NOT_STARTED, EXECUTING, SUCCESS, FAILED).\n\n---- Out of scope ------\n- Product search, shopping cart management, and order fulfillment.\n- Identity verification and KYC (Know Your Customer) processes.\n- Currency conversion processing (assumed handled by PSP).\n\n2) Scale Understanding \n- 1 million payment transactions per day.\n\n2) Non Functional Requirements (SPARCS):  \n- Scalability: System should easily handle 1M transactions/day and scale securely for peak e-commerce loads.\n- Performance: Focus is less on extreme low-latency throughput, and more on exact precision (e.g., using \"string\" data types for currency amounts to avoid rounding errors).\n- Availability: Highly available backend to accept checkout requests without dropping orders.\n- Reliability: Robust fault tolerance. Failed payments must be carefully handled and retried/logged without data loss.\n- Consistency: Strict ACID transaction support (Traditional Relational DB preferred over NoSQL). Asynchronous reconciliation process to keep internal services (accounting) and external services (PSPs) consistent.\n- Security: End-to-end traceability via an immutable Double-Entry Ledger system (sum of all entries must always equal 0).\n\n------- Out of Scope --------\n- Fraud detection and prevention.\n\n3) BOE Calculations \n- QPS\n    > Read QPS : 2*(1M transactions)/ (10^5) => 20 QPS\n    > Write QPS : 1*(1M transactions)/ (10^5) => 10 QPS\n    > Peak QPS ≈ 30 QPS (Write) / 60 QPS (Read)\n\n4) Core Entities\n- PaymentEvent (Tracks checkout_id, buyer/seller/card info, is_payment_done status)\n- PaymentOrder (Tracks payment_order_id, amount, currency, execution status, and ledger/wallet update flags)\n- Ledger (Double-entry system tracking Account, Debit, and Credit)\n\n5) API Design \n\nPOST /v1/payments -- Executes a payment event handling\n    Input: {buyer_info, checkout_id, credit_card_info, payment_orders}\n    Output: 2xx, success / accepted\n\nGET /v1/payments/:payment_order_id -- real-time execution status of payment order.\n    Output: {payment_order_id, execution_status, ledger_updated, wallet_updated, \nfailure_reason}\n\nPOST /v1/webhooks/psp-update -- asynchronous status updates from third-party PSP\n    Input: {event_type, psp_reference_id, payment_order_id, status}\n    Output: 200, success\n\nPOST /v1/payments/:payment_order_id/refund -- refund via a reverse double-entry ledger update\n    Input: {refund_amount, reason}\n    Output: 2xx, accepted\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
