    {
      "id": "aoui3fXAGxZFf4vIU88Wf",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -2394.4215123056456,
      "y": 3421.3812557693855,
      "width": 919.4992215988109,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eebefa",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bQ4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1744401587,
      "version": 1185,
      "versionNonce": 117911165,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781631038093,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) What Are RSS Feeds?\n- Imagine every news website had a standardized notice board where they automatically post\na short summary every time they publish a new story. Instead of you visiting 10,000\nwebsites to check for news, each site keeps its own tidy list that any program can read.\n- That \"standardized notice board\" is called an RSS feed (RSS stands for Really Simple\nSyndication).\n- An RSS feed is just a special file (written in a format called XML) that lists recent articles.\nAn RSS feed is designed for computers to read — it's plain, structured, and predictable.\n\n2) Crawling and Polling\n- Polling = regularly re-checking each RSS feed (e.g., \"every 5 minutes, look for new items\").\nSimple and effective.\n- Crawling = a program that visits webpages and reads their content directly. Used when a\npublisher doesn't offer an RSS feed.\n- Be respectful: good crawlers obey robots.txt (a file where sites say what's allowed) and\ndon't hammer servers too fast. Learn more from Google's crawling documentation.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) What Are RSS Feeds?\n- Imagine every news website had a standardized notice board where they automatically post a short summary every time they publish a new story. Instead of you visiting 10,000 websites to check for news, each site keeps its own tidy list that any program can read.\n- That \"standardized notice board\" is called an RSS feed (RSS stands for Really Simple Syndication).\n- An RSS feed is just a special file (written in a format called XML) that lists recent articles. An RSS feed is designed for computers to read — it's plain, structured, and predictable.\n\n2) Crawling and Polling\n- Polling = regularly re-checking each RSS feed (e.g., \"every 5 minutes, look for new items\"). Simple and effective.\n- Crawling = a program that visits webpages and reads their content directly. Used when a publisher doesn't offer an RSS feed.\n- Be respectful: good crawlers obey robots.txt (a file where sites say what's allowed) and don't hammer servers too fast. Learn more from Google's crawling documentation.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "2Or6gXU876BwqjoLWgqYw",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 302.6403313859105,
      "y": 1285.441819356869,
      "width": 916.2693684895834,
      "height": 2300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bQ5",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 249337693,
      "version": 11036,
      "versionNonce": 665823869,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781616763813,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) The payment flow is strictly divided into two operational phases to reflect real-world\nmoney movement.\n    > Pay-in \n    > Pay- out \n    > The platform (e.g., Amazon) acts as a temporary money custodian for a fee. Money\nlands in the platform's account first, and is later disbursed to sellers.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n2) Pay in Flow \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Payment Service: The central orchestrator.\n    > Receives payment events from user checkouts.\n    > Performs critical risk checks to prevent illegal activity. Usually delegated to specialized\n3rd-party risk providers.\n- Payment Executor: \n    > Handles the processing of individual payment orders. A single checkout event may be\nsplit into multiple distinct payment orders if buying from multiple sellers.\n- Payment Service Provider (PSP): \n    > An external entity (like Stripe or Square) that actually executes the money movement\nfrom the buyer's account to the platform's bank account.\n- Card Schemes: \n    > Organizations (Visa, MasterCard, Discover) that process the raw, underlying credit card\noperations within a highly complex ecosystem.\n- Wallet Service: \n    > Maintains the virtual account balance of the merchant. Tracks exactly how much the\nplatform owes the seller.\nLedger Service: The absolute financial system of record.\n    > The ledger keeps a financial record of the payment transaction.\n    > When a user pays the seller $1, we record it as debit $1 from a user and credit $1 to\nthe seller.\n    > Double-Entry Ledger: Serves as the immutable financial source of truth, ensuring every\ntransaction balances perfectly to zero (Debit = Credit) for absolute traceability and error\ndetection.\n- PCI Compliance & Hosted Pages: \n    > To bypass the massive regulatory burden of handling raw credit card data, the system\nuses PSP-provided widgets to tokenize card details on the client side, ensuring the backend\nnever touches sensitive data.\n\n3) Pay out flow \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Definition: Moving funds from the e-commerce platform's bank account to the individual\nseller's bank account.\n- Trigger: Initiated asynchronously only after specific business conditions are met (e.g.,\ndelivery confirmation, refund window closure, or an automated end-of-month schedule).\n- Providers: Utilizes specialized Accounts Payable (AP) providers (e.g., Tipalti, Stripe\nConnect) instead of standard consumer PSPs.\n- Complexity: Involves high regulatory friction (tax documentation like W-8/W-9, cross-border\nforeign exchange (FX), ongoing AML checks, and global routing rules).\n\n- Payout Service: The internal orchestrator that determines who to pay, when to pay, and\nhow much.\n- Accounts Payable (AP) Provider: The 3rd-party service that handles the actual\ndisbursement, tax compliance, and bank-to-bank routing.\n- Wallet Service: Validates that the seller has a sufficient positive balance available for\nwithdrawal.\n- Ledger Service: Records the final immutable reduction in platform liabilities (money officially\nleaving the ecosystem).",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) The payment flow is strictly divided into two operational phases to reflect real-world money movement.\n    > Pay-in \n    > Pay- out \n    > The platform (e.g., Amazon) acts as a temporary money custodian for a fee. Money lands in the platform's account first, and is later disbursed to sellers.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n2) Pay in Flow \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Payment Service: The central orchestrator.\n    > Receives payment events from user checkouts.\n    > Performs critical risk checks to prevent illegal activity. Usually delegated to specialized 3rd-party risk providers.\n- Payment Executor: \n    > Handles the processing of individual payment orders. A single checkout event may be split into multiple distinct payment orders if buying from multiple sellers.\n- Payment Service Provider (PSP): \n    > An external entity (like Stripe or Square) that actually executes the money movement from the buyer's account to the platform's bank account.\n- Card Schemes: \n    > Organizations (Visa, MasterCard, Discover) that process the raw, underlying credit card operations within a highly complex ecosystem.\n- Wallet Service: \n    > Maintains the virtual account balance of the merchant. Tracks exactly how much the platform owes the seller.\nLedger Service: The absolute financial system of record.\n    > The ledger keeps a financial record of the payment transaction.\n    > When a user pays the seller $1, we record it as debit $1 from a user and credit $1 to the seller.\n    > Double-Entry Ledger: Serves as the immutable financial source of truth, ensuring every transaction balances perfectly to zero (Debit = Credit) for absolute traceability and error detection.\n- PCI Compliance & Hosted Pages: \n    > To bypass the massive regulatory burden of handling raw credit card data, the system uses PSP-provided widgets to tokenize card details on the client side, ensuring the backend never touches sensitive data.\n\n3) Pay out flow \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Definition: Moving funds from the e-commerce platform's bank account to the individual seller's bank account.\n- Trigger: Initiated asynchronously only after specific business conditions are met (e.g., delivery confirmation, refund window closure, or an automated end-of-month schedule).\n- Providers: Utilizes specialized Accounts Payable (AP) providers (e.g., Tipalti, Stripe Connect) instead of standard consumer PSPs.\n- Complexity: Involves high regulatory friction (tax documentation like W-8/W-9, cross-border foreign exchange (FX), ongoing AML checks, and global routing rules).\n\n- Payout Service: The internal orchestrator that determines who to pay, when to pay, and how much.\n- Accounts Payable (AP) Provider: The 3rd-party service that handles the actual disbursement, tax compliance, and bank-to-bank routing.\n- Wallet Service: Validates that the seller has a sufficient positive balance available for withdrawal.\n- Ledger Service: Records the final immutable reduction in platform liabilities (money officially leaving the ecosystem).",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
